From 58fa3f36d0a11fe551afe72a4dd0300cad39ba01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 06:51:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Give the model face pixels on generated character shots
 too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The uploaded-source path has enforced a face-pixel floor since the
first repair; the character path never got it. Generated shots went to
the lip-sync model at whatever the video provider returned, which is
often 480p — and the model works on a small square crop around the
face, so that crop is starved before it starts. On a real sample the
synced mouth came back with about a sixth of the surrounding skin's
detail. Same defect, same fix, applied where it was missing.

Folded into trimClipToStart rather than added as a second pass. Every
shot already goes through that encode on its way to the model, and an
extra pass would be another generation of loss in exactly the region
the model is about to redraw. The upscale is capped at 2x for the same
reason the upload path caps it: past that we are inventing pixels
rather than recovering them, and h264's even-width rule and the
original framing both survive via scale=-2.

The length contract is unchanged and still asserted: a shot comes back
at exactly its scene duration whether or not it was upscaled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
